{"answer": "For local connections, peer authentication is used, which obtains the operating system user name from the kernel and checks if it matches the requested database user (peer-to-peer check). ident authentication is not used for local connections\u2014PostgreSQL substitutes peer instead.\n\nFor TCP/IP connections, ident authentication is used, which queries the client ident service on the connecting host to determine the operating system user name and checks if it matches the requested database user.\n\nSource: Chunk 5 (pg16_auth_006) \u2014 \"ident is only for TCP/IP and uses the client ident service; if ident is specified for a local connection, PostgreSQL uses peer instead. peer is available only for local connections.\"", "completion_tokens": 138, "evaluator_version": "eval_v1_model_runner", "latency_ms": 0, "prompt_tokens": 875, "query_id": "q_0008", "scores": {"correctness": 0.7, "overall": 0.7839333333333333, "support": 1.0}, "set_id": "q_0008_shuffled_order"}
